<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bracara Augusta</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Braga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bracara Augusta was a Roman city founded under Augustus around 16 BCE in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, on the site of modern Braga in Portugal. It became a centre of the Callaeci and, after the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, capital of the province of Gallaecia. In the 5th and 6th centuries it served as the chief seat of the Suebic kings and an important bishopric.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta.jpg" alt="Bracara Augusta" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Paraskevas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Braga_-_Cathedral_(Se)_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies in the heart of Minho, in a transitional region between east and west, between mountains, forests, valleys and plains. Human occupation there dates back thousands of years, with vestiges of monumental structures from the Megalithic era. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> the Castro culture extended into the northwest, and the Bracari occupied high ground in fortified settlements called castrum. The region became the domain of the Callaici Bracarii, a Celtic tribe who occupied what is now northern Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> began their conquest of the region around 136 BCE and finished it, by conquering the northern regions, in the reign of Augustus. The civitas was founded in 16 BCE and, in the administrative reorganization of the new acquisitions, Bracara was rededicated to the emperor and took the name Bracara Augusta. The city developed greatly during the 1st century and reached its maximum extent around the 2nd. Towards the end of the 3rd century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> promoted it to a capital within the newly founded Roman province of Gallaecia. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ausonius">Ausonius</a> described it as prosperous in the 4th century.</p><p>Between 402 and 470 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> conquered the area. In 410 they established a kingdom in the northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermeric">Hermeric</a> and lasting over 150 years, with Bracara as its capital. The old Roman amphitheatre was converted into a fortress. In 455 the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-ii">Theodoric II</a> sacked the city, destroying many historical and archaeological records, while the Suebi king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechiar">Rechiar</a> escaped, wounded, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a>. A list of dioceses and parishes of Braga made in 570 still exists, and by about 584 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> took permanent control of Gallaecia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site is the city of Braga in northwestern Portugal, capital of the district of Braga and of the historical Minho Province. It hosts the oldest Portuguese archdiocese, the Archdiocese of Braga, and is the seat of the Primacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spains</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a main centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. Archbishop Paio Mendes was a contributor to the recognition of Portugal as an independent kingdom under Afonso I Henriques in 1179, and the city was later handed to the Church's direct control, giving it the nickname Paiz Bracarense. The medieval castle in the city centre was destroyed in 1905.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Braga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The province was divided into seven judicial districts (conventus), centred on Tarraco, Carthago Nova, Caesaraugusta, Clunia, Asturica Augusta, Lucus Augusti and <b>Bracara Augusta</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom's roots lay in the 5th century, when the Suebi settled in the Roman province of Gallaecia, made <b>Bracara Augusta</b> (Braga) their capital and founded a kingdom known first as the Kingdom of the Suebi and later as the Kingdom of Galicia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-galicia">Kingdom of Galicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Theodoric II (453–466), sent into Hispania by the emperor Avitus, defeated the Suebic king Rechiarius on the river Órbigo in 456, sacked <b>Bracara Augusta</b> and took Baetica, Carthaginiensis and southern Lusitania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 28 May 1926 a military coup launched from <b>Braga</b> by General Manuel Gomes da Costa overthrew the First Portuguese Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/ditadura-nacional">Ditadura Nacional</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered the lands of the Callaeci and Astures, from the Douro to the Bay of Biscay, with <b>Bracara Augusta</b> (Braga) as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/gallaecia">Gallaecia</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom was centred on <b>Bracara Augusta</b> and ruled a Hispano-Roman majority through bishops and local elites.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-suebi">Kingdom of the Suebi</a></li></ul></section>
